  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered well to the east of the \nregion this afternoon with a trough digging down to our west. The \ntrough will dig down and then move into Arizona tonight. The high \nblocks the forward progression of the trough and we remain situated \nin between the two features through the weekend. As we head into next \nweek, the high starts to reestablish itself and shifts westward, \neventually settling over the desert southwest by midweek. Now that \nwe have the pattern established, let's talk about the details. \n\nTonight through Friday...scattered showers and thunderstorms will \ncontinue through this afternoon and into this evening, mainly along \na line from the Kaibab Plateau down through the Mogollon Rim to the \nWhite Mountains. Plenty of  moisture is across the region and storms \nhave been a pretty efficient rain producers. Flash flooding remains \nthe greatest threat through today. Even though storm motions have \nbeen faster (10-15 mph) we continue to see training/backbuilding of \nstorms, which only increases the potential for flash flooding as \nheavy rain becomes prolonged over one area. Shear is pretty weak at \nthe surface but strengthens quite a bit as you move aloft (as seen \non out 18Z sounding). Best instability and lift is over western \nArizona, closer to the trough axis, where the potential for strong \nto severe storms will remain possible. Expect a similar set up for \nFriday, with another round of scattered showers and thunderstorms \nand the potential for flash flooding. \n\nSaturday through Sunday...the pull between the ridge to the east and \nthe trough to the west will place us in more of a southwesterly \nflow, bringing in some drier air and warmer temperatures. Moisture \ndoesn't fully scour out and we may continue to see some isolated \nactivity, mainly confined to the White Mountains and the higher \nelevations. \n\nMonday and onward...The high starts to really make push back to the \nwest which may help to increase moisture across the region once \nagain. If the high is able to settle in a more favorable position, \nwe should start to see storm activity increase little by little each \nday. \n\nAnother day of scattered storms, then a downturn in activity into \nearly next week before we could see a return to a more active \nmonsoon. Plenty to look forward to in the coming week.",
